
7p Block Quadruple-Zeta Basis Set Archive
=========================================


Date: 4 Jun 2010


When publishing work that uses these basis sets, please use the following citation:

K.G. Dyall, in preparation. Basis sets are available from the Dirac web site, 
http://dirac.chem.sdu.dk.



This archive contains the exponents and the SCF spinor or orbital coefficients, 
given with respect to normalized (spherical or 2-spinor) Gaussian functions, for
the double-zeta basis sets described in the above article.  

NOTE: Functions are counted from the function with the smallest exponent to the largest. 

The SCF exponent sets were generated as follows:

* Optimization of the s, p, d, and f sets separately, on the total energy of the
  configuration average of the s^2 p^n configuration, to yield a 35s 35p 24d 14f
  set.
* Replacement of the outermost 2 SCF f exponents with the 6d correlating 3f
  exponent set, and reoptimization of the other 12 f exponents with the
  correlating f exponents fixed.
* Addition of the outer valence correlating f exponent to the SCF set without
  reoptimization

The resulting primitive basis set is a 35s 35p 24d 16f set. In all the following
descriptions, this is the set referred to as the SCF exponent set. 


The correlating exponents were generated as follows

* A 3d2f1g exponent set for 7s7p correlation was optimized in MR-SDCI calculations
  on the ground configuration, with 3 s and 3 p exponents from the SCF set
  included in the correlating space and held fixed.
* A 3f2g1h exponent set for 6d correlation was optimized in MR-SDCI calculations
  on the ground configuration, with double excitations from the 6d shell into
  the f shell coupled to J=0.
* The largest f exponent from the valence set was replaced by the smallest f 
  exponent from the 6d set, and the smallest valence f exponent was reoptimized.
* The valence correlating d set was replaced in the final selection with the SCF
  exponents.

The diffuse exponent sets were generated as follows.

* An s exponent and a p exponent were optimized in SCF calculations on the
  negative ion for Uut - Uus, with the remaining s and p exponents fixed
* A dfg exponent set was optimized in MR-SDCI calculations on the negative 
  ion for Uut - Uus, with 3 s and 3 p exponents from the SCF set, the correlating
  3d2f1g set, and the diffuse sp set included in the correlating space, i.e. a 
  4s4p4d3f2g correlating space.
* The s and p exponents were extrapolated in a geometric sequence from Uuh and Uus
  to obtain exponents for Uuo. The d, f, and g exponents for Uuo were determined by
  multiplying the smallest correlating exponent for Uuo by the ratio of the diffuse
  exponent to the smallest correlating exponent for Uus.



This archive contains the exponents and the SCF spinor or orbital coefficients,
given with respect to normalized (spherical or 2-spinor) Gaussian functions, for
the basis sets described in the above articles. The SCF exponent sets include 
the valence correlating d functions and f functions. The d functions are in fact
the SCF functions for the 6d.  The outermost two exponents of the f set were 
replaced by the core 6d correlating f exponents, and the remaining f exponents 
were reoptimized. The outermost of these f exponents was used for the valence
correlation, and the outermost valence f exponent was reoptimized with the other
f exponent held fixed. In all the following descriptions, this is the set 
referred to as the SCF exponent set. The diffuse functions were optimized in 
correlated calculations on the negative ion. 

The archive is set out as follows.

1. Exponents and SCF coefficients for Dirac-Hartree-Fock wave functions, in
   order of Z. The large component functions are given first, followed by the
   small component functions. Both components are labelled with the large
   component symmetry type, but the primitive functions for the small component
   are those obtained by restricted kinetic balance from the large component.  
   The spin-orbit components are labeled with "-" for j=l-1/2 and "+" for j=l+1/2.   

2. Exponents and SCF coefficients for spin-free modified Dirac-Hartree-Fock wave
   functions, in order of Z. The large component functions are given first, 
   followed by the pseudo-large component functions, then the Foldy-Wouthuysen
   transformed large component functions. Note that all components have the same
   symmetry type. 

3. Exponents of diffuse s, p, d, f, and g functions.

4. Exponents of valence and core correlating functions.



Basis set construction.
----------------------

Several different types of basis sets may be constructed by adding primitive
functions. These basis sets are described below.

NOTE: Functions are counted from the function with the smallest exponent to the largest. 

1. Valence flexibility. To the SCF functions, add the following:

   1st, 2nd, and 4th s primitives from the SCF set 
   2nd, 3rd, and 5th p primitives from the SCF set
   1st, 2nd, and 3rd d primitives from the SCF set
   1st and 2nd f primitives from the SCF set (correlating f primitives)
   valence correlating 1g

2. Valence + outer core. This set adds correlation of the 6d shell.
   To set 1, add the following:

   5th, and 6th s primitives from the SCF set 
   6th and 7th p primitives from the SCF set 
   4th d primitive from the SCF set 
   3rd and 4th f primitive from the SCF set (6d correlating f primitives)
   outer core correlating 2g1h

3. Diffuse basis: add the diffuse s, p, d, f, and g functions to any of the above
   basis sets.





